    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The season died by a thousand cuts, and the final ledger is ugly enough to demand a hard look in the mirror - Toronto finished 32-36-14, eighth in the Atlantic Division, with 78 points, 31 games behind, and a 15th-place seed that says the spring was over long before the calendar caught up. <strong>The most important development is that the Leafs' season is finished and the organization now has to decide whether this core can be repaired or must be reshaped.</strong> A seven-game losing streak at the end is no fluke; it is the sound of a team that never found enough goals, enough structure, or enough poise when the pressure tightened. The names on the roster will change, the staff may change, and the front office has to decide whether to reload around the existing spine or start moving pieces before free agency opens the door and the draft approaches. What lingers is not one bad night but a season's worth of missed chances and soft landings. <strong>Watch the coaching seat, the cap math, and whether Toronto finally chooses change over comfort.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 31, 2026"
      },
